---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import WorkedExample from '../components/WorkedExample.astro';
import { DIMENSION_LABELS, UNIT_GROUPS } from '../lib/calculations/units';

const title = 'How unit prices are calculated | CheaperPack';
const description =
  'The formula CheaperPack uses to compare package prices, the exact unit conversions behind it, and why it never compares weight with volume or converts currencies.';

const base = { mass: 'g', volume: 'ml', count: 'item' } as const;
const fmt = (n: number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: 10 }).format(n);
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="page pt-8 sm:pt-12"><div class="prose">
    <h1>How unit prices are calculated</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      CheaperPack does one simple thing carefully: it puts every pack in the same unit, divides price by size, and shows you
      the numbers it used.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>For each product:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Convert the package size to the unit you want to compare by, such as kilograms.</li>
      <li>Divide the price by that size. The result is the price per unit, for example the price per kilogram.</li>
      <li>The product with the lowest price per unit is the best value.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="formula">price per unit = price ÷ size in that unit</p>
    <p>
      For a reference amount such as 100 g, CheaperPack divides by the size in grams and multiplies by 100. Changing the
      reference amount (per kg, per 100 g, per lb or per oz) changes the numbers shown but never which product is cheapest.
    </p>

    <WorkedExample />

    <h2>What the differences mean</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Difference per unit:</strong> how much more a product costs per kg (or per litre, item and so on) than the
        best value, also shown as a percentage of the best value’s unit price.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Difference for the pack:</strong> how much less you would pay for the same amount at the best value’s unit
        price. This is the saving per pack, assuming you could buy that exact amount at the cheaper rate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ties:</strong> when products have exactly the same unit price, all of them are marked as the best value.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Unit conversions</h2>
    <p>
      CheaperPack uses exact, internationally defined conversion factors. Sizes are converted only within the same kind of
      measurement.
    </p>
    {
      UNIT_GROUPS.map((group) => (
        <>
          <h3>{DIMENSION_LABELS[group.dimension]}</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="data">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Unit</th>
                  <th scope="col">Equals</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {group.units.map((u) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>
                      1 {u.id === 'item' ? 'item' : u.symbol} <span class="unit-name">({u.label.replace(/^.*\(|\)$/g, '')})</span>
                    </td>
                    <td class="num">
                      {fmt(u.toBase)} {u.id === 'item' ? (u.toBase === 1 ? 'item' : 'items') : base[group.dimension]}
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </>
      ))
    }

    <h2>Why weight and volume aren’t compared</h2>
    <p>
      A gram is a unit of weight and a millilitre is a unit of volume. Turning one into the other needs the product’s
      density: a litre of water weighs about 1 kg, but a litre of cooking oil weighs about 0.92 kg and a litre of honey
      about 1.4 kg. Rather than guess, CheaperPack asks you to use the same kind of unit for every product. Many packs show
      both weight and volume, so you can usually pick one both labels share.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ounces need particular care: <strong>oz</strong> is a weight (28.35 g), while <strong>fl oz</strong> is a volume, and
      the US fluid ounce (29.57 ml) differs from the UK one (28.41 ml).
    </p>

    <h2>Why currencies aren’t converted</h2>
    <p>
      Exchange rates change constantly, and CheaperPack doesn’t connect to an exchange-rate service. So every product must
      be priced in the same currency. The currency setting changes how amounts are formatted, not their value.
    </p>

    <h2>Rounding</h2>
    <p>
      Calculations use the full precision of the numbers you enter. Results are rounded only for display, with extra
      decimal places for small unit prices (such as a price per gram) so that two different prices never look identical.
      The steps shown under each product are rounded too, so recalculating them by hand may differ in the last digit.
    </p>

    <h2>When the lowest unit price isn’t the best buy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You won’t use the whole pack before it expires.</li>
      <li>You don’t have room to store a large pack, or the money to spend up front.</li>
      <li>The products aren’t really equivalent, such as a different quality or concentration.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/tools/price-comparison/">Compare prices now</a> or <a href="/tools/">see all tools</a>.</p>
  </div></div>
</Base>

